course: dictionary
lesson: 2683
title: Dickens and the dickens
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A capital letter can change what a word means. Dickens, with a capital D, is a name: Charles Dickens, the famous English writer of long novels such as «Oliver Twist», «A Christmas Carol» and «Great Expectations».
explain: Lower-case «the dickens» is a different thing. It is an old-fashioned, mild word for the devil, used to show surprise or annoyance. «"What the dickens is that?"» means "what on earth is that?", not "what is that writer?"
explain: Context tells you which meaning you need. Look at the words around the word: if the sentence is about a book, a writer or a story, it is probably the name. If someone is surprised or cross, it is probably the old-fashioned word.
fact: Charles Dickens was a famous English writer, and his characters, such as Scrooge, Oliver and Pip, are read in many school English lessons.
fact: «Dickensian» describes poor, grim, crowded old places, or a story with many odd characters.
fact: «The dickens» is old-fashioned and mild. It is used to show surprise or annoyance, as in «What the dickens is that?»
fact: The idea that cheese gives you nightmares got a push from Scrooge blaming «a crumb of cheese» for a ghost, but there is no good evidence for it.
fact: Dickens is mostly a name, so it has few other uses. Check the capital letter and the sentence before you decide what it means.
exercise: «Dickens wrote «Oliver Twist» and «A Christmas Carol».» What does Dickens mean here? | A ghost in a cold, dark room | The devil | *A famous English writer of long novels | A grey, wet day
exercise: Grandad shouts, «What the dickens!» What does he mean? | He is talking about a book | He is eating cheese late at night | He is reading a novel by Dickens | *He is surprised or annoyed
exercise: «A street that feels Dickensian» is most like which? | Bright, new and empty | Wide blue sea and sand | *Poor, grim and crowded | Quiet hills of soft grass
test: Which character comes from a Dickens novel? | Peter Pan | *Scrooge | Sherlock Holmes | Harry Potter
test: Ella reads, «The dickens he is!» Which meaning fits best? | A long novel with a happy ending | A kind of cheese | *A mild, old-fashioned way to show surprise or annoyance | The writer Charles Dickens
exam: In «What the dickens is that?», what does dickens mean? | *A mild, old-fashioned word for the devil, used to show surprise | A poor, crowded city street | A famous writer of long novels | A long novel with many characters
